(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-09"] = {"15.1":{"s":"Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons with only single bonds, general formula CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. They are made industrially from crude oil and natural gas, and in the laboratory by hydrogenation of alkenes or alkynes, reduction of alkyl halides, the Wurtz reaction and Grignard reagents. They are fairly unreactive. They burn completely to carbon dioxide and water, crack on heating (pyrolysis), and can be reformed to aromatic compounds. They react with halogens by substitution in light.","f":[{"x":"C_nH_(2n+2) + ((3n + 1)/2)*O_2 -> n*CO_2 + (n + 1)*H_2O + heat","n":"Complete combustion of an alkane; n = number of carbon atoms.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 15.1, p.238"}],"w":["Halogenation of an alkane gives a mixture of all possible isomers and several substitution products.","Wurtz reaction doubles the carbon chain of the alkyl halide; it is best with identical halides."],"x":["Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons: all their C–C bonds are single, and the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ (methane CH₄, ethane C₂H₆). Their carbon atoms are sp³ hybridised, and the molecules are not planar. They are called paraffins, which means little affinity, because they do not react easily.","Preparation. Industrially from crude oil and natural gas. In the laboratory: (1) hydrogenation of an alkene or alkyne with H₂ and a Ni, Pt or Pd catalyst; (2) reduction of an alkyl halide with zinc and dilute acid; (3) the Wurtz reaction, 2R–X + 2Na (dry ether) → R–R + 2NaX, which gives an alkane with twice the carbon atoms of the halide; (4) a Grignard reagent with water, RMgX + H₂O → R–H + Mg(OH)X.","Reactions. Combustion: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ + (3n + 1)/2 O₂ → n CO₂ + (n + 1) H₂O, with much heat. Substitution by halogens in light or heat gives a mixture of products, CH₄ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + HCl and then further. Pyrolysis (cracking) breaks large alkanes into smaller alkanes and alkenes. Reforming turns alkanes into aromatic compounds at high temperature with a catalyst.","Boiling points rise with the number of carbons, and fall with branching. Common mistake: expecting the Wurtz reaction to work well with two different halides, which gives a mixture."],"e":"Wurtz reaction with bromoethane: 2C₂H₅Br + 2Na → C₄H₁₀ + 2NaBr. The product is butane, with twice the carbon atoms of the halide."},"15.2":{"s":"Alkenes have a C=C double bond, general formula CₙH₂ₙ, and are unsaturated, so they undergo addition reactions. They add hydrogen, halogens and hydrogen halides; the addition of HX to an unsymmetrical alkene follows Markovnikov's rule: the negative part goes to the carbon with fewer hydrogens. With peroxides, HBr adds the opposite way (Kharasch effect). Ozonolysis breaks the double bond and gives carbonyl compounds, which locates the position of the bond.","f":[{"x":"Alkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ ; alkene CₙH₂ₙ ; alkyne CₙH₂ₙ₋₂","n":"General formulae of hydrocarbons. Open chain, one double or triple bond for alkene or alkyne."},{"x":"CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃–CH₃","n":"Hydrogenation of alkenes. Ni, Pt or Pd catalyst (Sabatier–Senderens)."},{"x":"HBr + peroxide: Br adds to the C with more H atoms","n":"Anti-Markovnikov (peroxide effect)"}],"w":["Markovnikov's rule: the hydrogen goes to the carbon that already has more hydrogens.","The peroxide effect works only for HBr, not for HCl or HI."],"x":["Alkenes have a carbon–carbon double bond, and the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ (ethene C₂H₄, propene C₃H₆). The carbons of the double bond are sp² hybridised, with a planar shape. The double bond has one sigma and one pi bond, and the pi bond makes alkenes unsaturated and reactive.","Preparation. By dehydration of alcohols with concentrated H₂SO₄ (ethanol gives ethene at about 443 K), by dehydrohalogenation of alkyl halides with alcoholic KOH, and by partial hydrogenation of alkynes.","Reactions. Alkenes undergo addition. They add H₂ (with a Ni catalyst), halogens (bromine water is decolourised, a test for unsaturation) and hydrogen halides. Markovnikov's rule: when HX adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, the hydrogen goes to the carbon that already has more hydrogens, and the halide to the carbon with fewer. So propene and HBr give 2-bromopropane. In the presence of peroxides, HBr adds in the opposite way (the Kharasch effect) to give 1-bromopropane. Only HBr shows this effect.","Ozonolysis: the alkene reacts with ozone, and then zinc and water break the double bond and give aldehydes or ketones. This locates the position of the double bond. Alkenes also burn, and are oxidised by cold dilute KMnO₄ to diols (the Baeyer test)."],"e":"Ozonolysis of propene, CH₃CH=CH₂, gives ethanal (CH₃CHO) and methanal (HCHO). Each carbon of the double bond becomes a C=O group."},"15.3":{"s":"Alkynes have a C≡C triple bond, general formula CₙH₂ₙ₋₂. Ethyne (acetylene) is the simplest. They are made by dehydrohalogenation of dihalides and from calcium carbide. They add hydrogen, halogens and hydrogen halides in two steps, following Markovnikov's rule. Addition of water in the presence of mercury(II) salts gives a carbonyl compound by tautomerism: ethyne gives ethanal, and other alkynes give ketones. Acetylene is used for welding.","f":[{"x":"Alkane CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ ; alkene CₙH₂ₙ ; alkyne CₙH₂ₙ₋₂","n":"General formulae of hydrocarbons. Open chain, one double or triple bond for alkene or alkyne."}],"w":["Water adds to ethyne to give ethanal, but to every other alkyne it gives a ketone.","Terminal alkynes (with a C≡C–H) are weakly acidic; alkenes are not."],"x":["Alkynes have a carbon–carbon triple bond, and the general formula is CₙH₂ₙ₋₂. The simplest is ethyne (acetylene), C₂H₂. The triple bond carbons are sp hybridised, and the shape is linear. The triple bond has one sigma and two pi bonds.","Preparation. Ethyne is made from calcium carbide and water, CaC₂ + 2H₂O → C₂H₂ + Ca(OH)₂. Alkynes are also made by dehydrohalogenation of dihalides with alcoholic KOH (and sodamide).","Reactions. Alkynes add H₂, halogens and hydrogen halides, in two steps, and Markovnikov's rule applies to unsymmetrical additions. Addition of water with mercury(II) sulfate and dilute H₂SO₄ gives an enol, which at once changes into a carbonyl compound. Ethyne gives ethanal, and every other alkyne gives a ketone, as propyne gives propanone.","Acidic nature. A hydrogen on a triple-bond carbon is weakly acidic, so terminal alkynes (with the group C≡C–H) react with sodium and with ammoniacal silver nitrate. Alkenes and alkanes do not. Uses: ethyne is burned in oxy-acetylene torches for welding, and it is a raw material for making many chemicals."],"e":"Distinguish propyne from but-2-yne. Propyne has a terminal C≡C–H, so it gives a white precipitate with ammoniacal silver nitrate. But-2-yne has no such hydrogen, so there is no precipitate."},"15.4":{"s":"Aromatic hydrocarbons, or arenes, contain a benzene ring. Benzene, C₆H₆, is a planar ring with six equal C–C bonds, a resonance hybrid of the two Kekulé structures. It resists addition and prefers substitution, as in nitration, halogenation and Friedel-Crafts reactions. It burns with a sooty flame and does not decolourise bromine water or alkaline KMnO₄. Substituents on the ring direct a new group to the ortho and para or to the meta position.","f":[{"x":"Planar, cyclic, conjugated ring with (4n + 2) π electrons, n = 0, 1, 2…","n":"Hückel rule for aromaticity. Benzene has 6 π electrons (n = 1)."}],"w":["Benzene has all C–C bond lengths equal (139 pm): it is not three single and three double bonds.","Benzene is carcinogenic and toxic; handle it only with care."],"x":["Aromatic hydrocarbons (arenes) contain a benzene ring. Benzene, C₆H₆, is planar, with six carbon atoms in a regular hexagon, six equal C–C bonds (139 pm, between the single 154 pm and double 134 pm values), and a cloud of six delocalised pi electrons. It is a resonance hybrid of two Kekulé structures, and this gives it extra stability.","Because of this stability, benzene does not behave like an alkene. It resists addition, does not decolourise bromine water or alkaline KMnO₄, and prefers substitution, in which a hydrogen is replaced and the ring stays. Examples: nitration with a mix of concentrated HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ gives nitrobenzene; halogenation with Cl₂ and a catalyst such as FeCl₃ gives chlorobenzene; sulfonation with fuming H₂SO₄ gives benzenesulfonic acid; Friedel–Crafts alkylation and acylation use an alkyl or acyl halide with anhydrous AlCl₃.","Directing effects. Groups such as –OH, –NH₂ and –CH₃ activate the ring and direct the new group to the ortho and para positions. Groups such as –NO₂ and –COOH deactivate the ring and direct the new group to the meta position.","Benzene burns with a sooty flame, because of its high carbon content. It is toxic and carcinogenic, so it must be handled with great care."],"e":"Nitrate toluene. The methyl group directs to the ortho and para positions, so the main products are 2-nitrotoluene and 4-nitrotoluene. Nitrobenzene, in contrast, gives mainly the meta product on further nitration."}};
